# Maintenance

## Objective

Favor changes that reduce long-term maintenance burden: remove dead code, fix root causes, avoid accumulating quick patches.

## Core Principles

- Fix the root cause of a bug, not just the symptom, when feasible within scope.
- Remove code made dead/unreachable by the current change instead of leaving it behind.
- Avoid stacking quick patches on top of quick patches — flag when a real refactor is warranted instead.
- Keep TODOs actionable and time-bound, not permanent markers nobody will revisit.

## Requirements

- When fixing a bug, check whether it stems from a deeper issue worth addressing, and say so even if out of scope.
- Delete code paths that become unreachable as a result of the current change.
- If a section clearly needs a larger refactor rather than another patch, flag it explicitly rather than adding to the pile.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Patching a symptom repeatedly instead of ever addressing the underlying root cause.
- Leaving now-unreachable code 'in case it's needed again'.
- Adding a vague `// TODO: fix this later` with no context or plan.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Root cause was addressed where feasible, not just the surface symptom.
✓ Code made dead by this change was removed.
✓ Any deferred larger fix is flagged explicitly, not silently left as debt.

## Golden Rule

Every change is a chance to leave the codebase slightly healthier, not just slightly different.
